## 7. The RFC process — when you stop coding and ask

A small number of changes touch shared surfaces and **cannot be made by one agent alone**, because they affect every other agent's mental model. For these, the agent pauses, writes a short RFC into §11.3 of this doc, and waits for a second pair of eyes (human maintainer or another agent with `review:` permission) before proceeding.

### 7.1 What requires an RFC

| Change | Why it needs an RFC |
|---|---|
| Adding a new port (`core/ports/*`) | Every feature's `wire(**deps)` signature changes shape. |
| Adding a new event to `core/events/catalog.py` | Every subscriber's type surface changes; FE bus mirror must also update. |
| Changing the shape of an existing event | Same as above, but worse — all publishers AND subscribers must update in lock-step. |
| Adding a field to a domain type in `packages/contracts/schemas/*` | The whole TS codegen pipeline updates; stale generated types fail CI everywhere. |
| Adding a new adapter in `infrastructure/<port>/` | Not gated — but the composition-root wiring change that binds it to a port IS gated, because it changes production behavior. |
| Changing `composition_root.py` in a way that re-binds an existing port | Every feature's runtime dependency changes. |
| Changing which adapter is bound for `prod` vs `test` | Affects every integration test. |
| Adding a top-level dependency (new pip package, new bun package, new vendor SDK) | Explicit rule in `CLAUDE.md §10` — stop and ask. |
| Deviating from a "locked" decision in `01-strategy.md §7` | Explicit rule in `CLAUDE.md §10` — stop and ask. |
| Changing the deployment target (e.g. swapping HF Spaces for Fly.io) | Affects `deploy.yml`, secrets surface, and every downstream agent's assumption. |

### 7.2 What does NOT require an RFC

| Change | Why it's agent-local |
|---|---|
| Any file inside your claimed `features/<name>/` | Your folder. Own it. |
| Prompt edits to your feature's `prompts.py` | Per-feature, zero outside callers. |
| Adding a private file (helper, parser, ranker) inside your feature | Private is private. |
| Adding a feature-local test file | Local. |
| Adding a FE primitive under `shared/ui/` if it's truly shared | Edge case — if in doubt, co-locate in your feature until a second feature needs it. Premature sharing is a coupling smell. |
| Tightening a Pydantic validator on an existing schema field | If the shape doesn't change, no RFC. |
| Bug fixes in the composition root that don't re-bind ports | E.g. fixing a typo in a log line. |

### 7.3 The RFC template

Short. Two paragraphs, not two pages. Dropped into §11.3 as:

```
### RFC-NNNN · <short title>  ·  <date> · <agent>

**What**: one sentence — the concrete change.
**Why**: one paragraph — the forcing function. What does this unlock that cannot be done otherwise?
**Blast radius**: which features are affected (by name), which docs need updating in the same PR, which tripwires will flip red until the change lands.
**Alternatives considered**: one line each, at least two.
**Decision**: filled in by reviewer. `accepted | rejected | deferred`.
```

If an agent cannot write the "Why" without saying *"because my feature needs it"*, the agent is probably doing the wrong thing at the feature boundary. The fix is usually inside their folder, not in the shared surface.

### 7.4 Example RFCs (reference, don't implement)

**RFC-0001 · Add `TracingPort` to enable per-feature spans.**
**Why**: on demo day we want `feature=voice` to show end-to-end latency in logs; `structlog` gives us free-text, but we want structured spans for debugging.
**Blast radius**: every `wire(**deps)` adds `tracer`. Every use-case decorates its top method. `composition_root.py` binds a `StructlogTracer` adapter. No feature logic changes.
**Alternatives**: (a) do nothing, use grep over logs — rejected, too slow during demo prep; (b) use OTel — rejected, `05-merged-plan.md §5` drops OTel day 1.
**Decision**: deferred to post-hack, just use `structlog.bind(feature=...)` for now.

**RFC-0002 · Add `briefing.regenerated` event.**
**Why**: when `span_locker` rejects a briefing and the translator retries, we want the UI to show a toast. Currently the second `briefing.generated` overwrites the first silently.
**Blast radius**: `core/events/catalog.py` (+1 dataclass), FE bus mirror (+1 entry), `features/translator/use_case.py` (publish on retry), `features/feed/sse.py` (re-emit on SSE).
**Alternatives**: (a) piggyback a boolean `was_retry` on `briefing.generated` — rejected, mutates event shape; (b) do nothing — rejected, the toast is user-visible.
**Decision**: accepted.

These are illustrative. Real RFCs go into §11.3.

